      <h2>Website Structure</h2>

      <p>Every time you open a website, you're looking at a carefully organized collection of files, folders, and code
        that has been designed and arranged to deliver information in a meaningful way. The <strong>structure of a
          website</strong> is the invisible skeleton that holds everything together — from the way files are organized
        on a server, to the way content is laid out on a page, to the way different pages connect with each other.</p>

      <p>Think of a website like a well-organized office building. The building has a clear address (the domain), rooms
        for different departments (pages), a directory at the entrance (navigation), filing cabinets full of documents
        (files and databases), and an overall architectural plan that keeps everything functional and accessible.
        Without structure, visitors get lost, search engines can't find content, and developers struggle to maintain or
        update anything.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Analogy:</strong> A website without structure is like a library where someone dumped every book in a
        pile on the floor with no labels, shelves, or catalog. It might contain all the information in the world — but
        nobody can find anything.
      </div>

      <h3>1.1 The Three Foundational Layers</h3>
      <p>Every modern website is built on three distinct technology layers that work together like a perfectly
        coordinated team:</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>🏗 HTML — Structure</h4>
          <p><strong>HyperText Markup Language</strong> is the skeleton of a webpage. It defines content — headings,
            paragraphs, images, links, tables, and forms — using <em>tags</em> like <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>,
            <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>. It tells the browser <em>what</em> to display.</p>
          <span class="badge">Content Layer</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>🎨 CSS — Presentation</h4>
          <p><strong>Cascading Style Sheets</strong> is the clothing and skin. It controls colors, fonts, spacing,
            layout, and animations. It tells the browser <em>how</em> content should look.</p>
          <span class="badge badge-purple">Style Layer</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>⚙️ JavaScript — Behavior</h4>
          <p><strong>JavaScript</strong> is the muscles and brain. It adds interactivity — dropdown menus, form
            validation, live updates, animations, and dynamic content. It tells the browser <em>what to do</em> when
            users interact.</p>
          <span class="badge badge-success">Logic Layer</span>
        </div>
      </div>

