      <h3>1.3 File &amp; Folder Structure</h3>
      <p>Beyond individual pages, a website is a <strong>collection of organized files and folders</strong> stored on a
        server. A professional project typically follows conventions to keep everything tidy and maintainable:</p>

      <pre>/my-website/
├── index.html          ← Homepage (default page loaded)
├── about.html          ← About page
├── contact.html        ← Contact page
├── /css/
│   ├── style.css       ← Main stylesheet
│   └── responsive.css  ← Mobile/tablet styles
├── /js/
│   ├── main.js         ← Core scripts
│   └── analytics.js    ← Tracking scripts
├── /images/
│   ├── logo.png
│   └── hero-banner.jpg
├── /fonts/
└── /assets/            ← Downloads, PDFs, etc.
  </pre>

      <div class="callout callout-tip">
        <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> The file named <code>index.html</code> is treated as the default file by most web
        servers. When someone visits <code>https://example.com</code>, the server automatically serves
        <code>index.html</code> without needing the full URL.
      </div>

      <h3>1.4 Types of Website Structures</h3>
      <p>Websites organize their pages in different structural patterns depending on their purpose and size:</p>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Hierarchical (Tree)</h4>
          <p>Most common. A homepage branches into categories, then into individual pages. Like a corporate org-chart.
            Example: large news sites, e-commerce platforms.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Linear (Sequential)</h4>
          <p>Pages flow one after another in order. Used for tutorials, courses, slideshows. Users move Forward/Back
            with no branching.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Webbed (Hyperlink Network)</h4>
          <p>Pages interconnect freely in all directions. Used in wikis, research databases. Very flexible but can
            disorient users.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Database-Driven</h4>
          <p>Content is stored in a database and pages are generated dynamically. Used in blogs (WordPress), social
            platforms, e-commerce. One template generates thousands of pages.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>1.5 Static vs Dynamic Websites</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Feature</th>
              <th>Static Website</th>
              <th>Dynamic Website</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Content</td>
              <td>Fixed HTML files</td>
              <td>Generated from database on each request</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Speed</td>
              <td>Very fast (pre-built)</td>
              <td>Slightly slower (real-time generation)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Maintenance</td>
              <td>Edit files manually</td>
              <td>Update database, CMS handles display</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Cost</td>
              <td>Very cheap to host</td>
              <td>Requires server-side processing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Examples</td>
              <td>Portfolio, landing page, docs</td>
              <td>Blog, e-commerce, social network</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Technologies</td>
              <td>HTML, CSS, JS only</td>
              <td>PHP, Python, Node.js + Database</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>1.6 Navigation Structure</h3>
      <p>Navigation is the roadmap of a website. It determines how easily users can find what they need. Poor navigation
        is the #1 reason users abandon websites. Key types include:</p>
      <ul class="styled">
        <li><strong>Primary Navigation:</strong> The main menu — usually in the header. Highlights the most important
          sections.</li>
        <li><strong>Secondary Navigation:</strong> Sidebar or sub-menus for deeper sections within a category.</li>
        <li><strong>Breadcrumbs:</strong> A trail showing where the user is (Home → Electronics → Laptops → Dell XPS).
          Borrowed from Hansel and Gretel.</li>
        <li><strong>Footer Navigation:</strong> Links to legal pages, sitemaps, contact info. Often overlooked but
          important.</li>
        <li><strong>Sitemap:</strong> An XML file (for search engines) or HTML page (for users) listing all pages. Helps
          Google crawl and index the site.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>1.7 URL Structure — Anatomy of a Web Address</h3>
      <p>Every page has a unique URL (Uniform Resource Locator). Understanding URL structure helps you understand
        website architecture:</p>

      <!-- DIAGRAM: URL Anatomy -->
      <div class="diagram-wrap">
        <div class="diagram-title">Anatomy of a URL</div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 680 130" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="100%" style="max-width:680px;display:block;margin:auto;">
          <!-- URL text -->
          <text x="340" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="15" fill="var(--text-primary)" letter-spacing="1">https://www.example.com/blog/post-1</text>
