    <section class="section" id="domain">
      <div class="section-label">Section 02</div>
      <h2>Domain Names</h2>

      <p>Every website on the internet lives at an <strong>IP address</strong> — a numerical address like
        <code>142.250.80.46</code>. Remembering such numbers is hard for humans, so the <strong>Domain Name System
          (DNS)</strong> was invented. DNS is essentially the internet's phonebook. It translates human-friendly names
        like <code>google.com</code> into machine-friendly IP addresses, silently and instantly, every time you visit a
        website.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Analogy:</strong> A domain name is like your home's street address on Google Maps. Your actual GPS
        coordinates (IP address) are hard to remember, but "123 Main Street, Springfield" is easy. When you tell someone
        your address, they look it up in the maps system (DNS) to find the exact location.
      </div>

      <h3>2.1 History of Domain Names</h3>
      <p>In the very early internet (ARPANET, 1970s), computers had to maintain a manual text file called
        <strong>HOSTS.TXT</strong> that mapped computer names to IP addresses. As the network grew to hundreds, then
        thousands of computers, this became completely unmanageable — every new computer meant updating the file on
        every other computer in the network.</p>
      <p>In <strong>1983</strong>, Paul Mockapetris invented DNS, a distributed, hierarchical, automated system that
        scales to billions of entries. The first domain name ever registered was <code>symbolics.com</code> on March 15,
        1985. Today, there are over <strong>350 million registered domain names</strong> worldwide.</p>

      <h3>2.2 Anatomy of a Domain Name</h3>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Top-Level Domain (TLD)</h4>
          <p>The rightmost part: <code>.com</code>, <code>.org</code>, <code>.net</code>, <code>.edu</code>,
            <code>.gov</code>. Country-code TLDs include <code>.in</code>, <code>.uk</code>, <code>.de</code>. New
            generic TLDs: <code>.app</code>, <code>.design</code>, <code>.shop</code>.</p>
          <span class="badge badge-success">Managed by ICANN</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Second-Level Domain (SLD)</h4>
          <p>The actual name you register: <code>google</code> in <code>google.com</code>, <code>wikipedia</code> in
            <code>wikipedia.org</code>. This is the part you own and control.</p>
          <span class="badge">Your Brand</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Subdomain</h4>
          <p>A prefix before the domain: <code>blog.example.com</code>, <code>shop.example.com</code>,
            <code>api.example.com</code>. You create these yourself. <code>www</code> is the most traditional subdomain.
          </p>
          <span class="badge badge-purple">You Control This</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>2.3 How DNS Works — Step by Step</h3>
      <p>When you type a URL into your browser, a fascinating chain of lookups happens in milliseconds:</p>
      <div class="step-list">
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">01</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Browser Cache Check</h5>
            <p>Your browser first checks if it recently looked up this domain. If yes, it uses the cached IP address
              immediately — no further lookup needed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">02</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Operating System / Router Cache</h5>
            <p>If the browser cache misses, it asks your OS and then your router if they have a recent record.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">03</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Recursive Resolver (Your ISP)</h5>
            <p>Your Internet Service Provider runs a DNS Resolver. If it doesn't have the answer cached, it starts
              asking other DNS servers on your behalf.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">04</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Root Name Server</h5>
            <p>The resolver asks one of 13 root name servers (distributed globally). The root server says "For
              <code>.com</code>, ask the Verisign name server."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">05</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>TLD Name Server</h5>
            <p>The TLD server for <code>.com</code> says "For <code>google.com</code>, ask Google's authoritative name
              server."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">06</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Authoritative Name Server</h5>
            <p>Google's own name server finally answers: "The IP for <code>google.com</code> is
              <code>142.250.80.46</code>." This answer flows back through the chain.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">07</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Connection Established</h5>
            <p>Your browser now has the IP address and connects directly to Google's server. The whole process takes
              under 50 milliseconds.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

