      <h3>2.4 Types of Domain Extensions (TLDs)</h3>
      <ul class="styled">
        <li><strong>.com</strong> — Commercial. The most popular and trusted TLD globally. Originally for businesses.
        </li>
        <li><strong>.org</strong> — Organizations. Commonly used by non-profits, open source projects.</li>
        <li><strong>.net</strong> — Network. Originally for network infrastructure companies, now general purpose.</li>
        <li><strong>.edu</strong> — Education. Restricted to accredited educational institutions.</li>
        <li><strong>.gov</strong> — Government. Strictly for US government agencies only.</li>
        <li><strong>.io</strong> — Originally Indian Ocean territory. Became hugely popular for tech startups.</li>
        <li><strong>Country codes:</strong> .in (India), .uk (United Kingdom), .de (Germany), .jp (Japan).</li>
        <li><strong>New gTLDs:</strong> .app, .design, .store, .tech, .blog — hundreds registered in 2014–present.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2.5 Registering a Domain Name</h3>
      <p>Domain names are registered through accredited <strong>registrars</strong> — companies authorized by ICANN
        (Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers) to sell domain registrations. Popular registrars include
        GoDaddy, Namecheap, Google Domains, and Cloudflare.</p>
      <div class="callout callout-warn">
        <strong>Important:</strong> You never truly <em>own</em> a domain name. You <em>lease</em> it for 1–10 years and
        must renew it. Forgetting to renew can mean losing your domain to someone else — which has happened to major
        companies.
      </div>

      <h3>2.6 DNS Record Types</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
