    <section class="section" id="hosting">
      <div class="section-label">Section 03</div>
      <h2>Web Hosting</h2>

      <p>If a domain name is your website's address, then <strong>web hosting</strong> is the actual physical (or
        virtual) space where your website lives. Web hosting is a service that provides space on a server — a powerful
        computer that's always connected to the internet — so that your website's files (HTML, CSS, images, databases)
        are accessible to visitors 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, 365 days a year.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Analogy:</strong> Think of web hosting like renting office space in a building. The building is a data
        center (a facility full of powerful computers). Your office is your "slice" of server space. Your furniture and
        equipment are your website files. The building manager keeps the power on, the internet connected, and the
        security running so your office is always open for clients.
      </div>

      <h3>3.1 How Web Hosting Works</h3>
      <div class="step-list">
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">01</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Upload Your Files</h5>
            <p>You upload your website files (HTML, CSS, images, etc.) to your hosting account's server, usually via FTP
              (File Transfer Protocol) or a hosting control panel like cPanel.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">02</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Server Stays Online</h5>
            <p>The hosting provider's server runs 24/7, powered by backup generators, cooled in climate-controlled data
              centers, and connected to multiple redundant internet lines.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">03</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Visitor Requests Your Site</h5>
            <p>When someone types your domain into their browser, DNS translates it to your server's IP, and the browser
              sends an HTTP request to your server.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">04</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Server Responds</h5>
            <p>Your server processes the request and sends back the HTML, CSS, and other files. The browser assembles
              them into the visible webpage.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

