      <h3>3.2 Types of Web Hosting</h3>

      <h4>Shared Hosting</h4>
      <p>Multiple websites share the same physical server and its resources (CPU, RAM, disk space). It's the cheapest
        option and perfect for beginners, small blogs, and low-traffic sites. The downside: if a neighboring site gets a
        traffic spike, it can slow everyone on that server down — the "noisy neighbor" problem.</p>
      <div class="card">
        <strong>Best for:</strong> Personal blogs, portfolio sites, small business pages<br>
        <strong>Examples:</strong> Bluehost, HostGator, SiteGround shared plans<br>
        <strong>Bandwidth:</strong> Usually 5–20 GB/month | <span class="badge">Entry Level</span>
      </div>

      <h4>Virtual Private Server (VPS) Hosting</h4>
      <p>A physical server is divided into multiple virtual servers using virtualization software. Each VPS has
        <strong>dedicated resources</strong> (guaranteed RAM and CPU) and runs its own operating system. It's like
        owning a condo in a building — you have your own space and utilities, but share the building infrastructure.</p>
      <div class="card">
        <strong>Best for:</strong> Growing websites, developers, medium traffic<br>
        <strong>Examples:</strong> DigitalOcean, Linode, Vultr, Hostinger VPS<br>
        <strong>Control:</strong> Root access | <span class="badge badge-purple">Mid Level</span>
      </div>

      <h4>Dedicated Server Hosting</h4>
      <p>You rent an <strong>entire physical server</strong> exclusively for your website. Maximum performance, complete
        control, maximum cost. Used by large e-commerce platforms, gaming servers, and enterprises. Like owning an
        entire building.</p>
      <div class="card">
        <strong>Best for:</strong> High-traffic sites, large enterprises, sensitive data<br>
        <strong>Examples:</strong> Liquid Web, Rackspace, OVHcloud<br>
        <span class="badge badge-success">Enterprise Level</span>
      </div>

      <h4>Cloud Hosting</h4>
      <p>Your website runs across a <strong>network of interconnected servers</strong>. Resources scale dynamically up
        or down based on traffic. If one server fails, others instantly take over. This is the most resilient and
        scalable option. Major providers include AWS, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure.</p>
      <div class="card">
        <strong>Best for:</strong> Rapidly growing startups, variable traffic, global audience<br>
        <strong>Examples:</strong> AWS EC2, Google Cloud Run, Azure App Service<br>
        <strong>Advantage:</strong> Pay only for what you use | <span class="badge badge-warn">Scalable</span>
      </div>

      <h4>Managed WordPress Hosting</h4>
      <p>Specialized hosting tuned specifically for WordPress sites. The host handles WordPress updates, security
        patches, backups, and caching automatically. Premium price, zero technical headache.</p>

      <h4>Static Site Hosting</h4>
      <p>For websites with no backend or database (pure HTML/CSS/JS). Extremely fast, cheap (often free), and secure
        because there's no server-side code to exploit. Examples: Netlify, Vercel, GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages.</p>

      <h3>3.3 Key Hosting Metrics to Evaluate</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Metric</th>
              <th>What It Means</th>
              <th>Good Value</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Uptime</td>
              <td>% of time server is online and accessible</td>
              <td>99.9% or higher</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Bandwidth</td>
              <td>Data transferred per month (page loads × visitors)</td>
              <td>Unmetered for most sites</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Storage</td>
              <td>Disk space for files, images, databases</td>
              <td>10 GB+ for typical site</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Response Time</td>
              <td>Time server takes to respond to a request (TTFB)</td>
              <td>Under 200ms</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Support</td>
              <td>24/7 availability of technical help</td>
              <td>Live chat + phone</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Backups</td>
              <td>Automated copies of your site</td>
              <td>Daily, with 30-day retention</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout callout-info">
        <strong>99.9% Uptime = 8.7 hours of downtime per year.</strong> 99.99% = ~52 minutes/year. 99.999% ("five
        nines") = ~5 minutes/year. Enterprise systems often target five nines.
      </div>

      <h3>3.4 Control Panels — cPanel, Plesk &amp; Others</h3>
      <p>Most shared and VPS hosting comes with a <strong>control panel</strong> — a web-based interface for managing
        your hosting without using command-line tools. Through a control panel you can create email accounts, manage
        databases, install WordPress with one click, view site statistics, configure security settings, and manage
        files.</p>
      <p><strong>cPanel</strong> is the most popular (Linux servers). <strong>Plesk</strong> works on both Linux and
        Windows. Modern alternatives include DirectAdmin and Froxlor.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════
     SECTION 4 — SSL CERTIFICATES
════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="section" id="ssl">
      <div class="section-label">Section 04</div>
      <h2>SSL Certificates &amp; HTTPS</h2>

      <p>Have you ever noticed the <strong>padlock icon</strong> in your browser's address bar? That small symbol
        represents one of the most important security technologies on the web — the <strong>SSL/TLS
          Certificate</strong>. It means all data exchanged between your browser and the website is <em>encrypted</em>
        and cannot be read by anyone intercepting the connection.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Analogy:</strong> Without SSL, sending data over the internet is like sending a postcard — anyone who
        handles it can read it. With SSL, it's like sending a letter in a sealed envelope that can only be opened with a
        unique key held exclusively by the recipient. Even if someone intercepts the envelope, they see only gibberish.
      </div>

      <h3>4.1 What Does SSL Actually Mean?</h3>
      <p><strong>SSL</strong> stands for <em>Secure Sockets Layer</em>. It was developed by Netscape in 1995. Over the
        years it was superseded by <strong>TLS</strong> (Transport Layer Security), which is what's actually used today
        (TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3). However, "SSL" stuck as the common term, so when someone says SSL certificate, they
        almost always mean a TLS certificate.</p>
      <p>When a website has a valid SSL certificate, its URL begins with <code>https://</code> instead of
        <code>http://</code>. The "S" stands for <strong>Secure</strong>. Without it, browsers like Chrome now display
        "Not Secure" warnings — a major deterrent for visitors.</p>

