      <h3>4.2 How SSL/TLS Works — The Handshake</h3>
      <p>Before any encrypted data is exchanged, a browser and server perform a <strong>TLS Handshake</strong> — a rapid
        negotiation in milliseconds that establishes a secure connection:</p>

      <div class="step-list">
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">01</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Client Hello</h5>
            <p>Your browser says: "I want to connect securely. Here are the TLS versions and encryption algorithms I
              support."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">02</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Server Hello + Certificate</h5>
            <p>The server responds: "Great. Let's use TLS 1.3 with AES encryption. Here's my SSL certificate to prove
              who I am."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">03</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Certificate Verification</h5>
            <p>Your browser checks the certificate against a list of trusted Certificate Authorities (CAs). If it's
              valid and not expired, trust is established.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">04</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Session Keys Generated</h5>
            <p>Both sides use asymmetric cryptography (public/private key pairs) to securely agree on a shared
              <em>session key</em> — unique to this connection.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">05</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Encrypted Communication</h5>
            <p>All further data uses the faster symmetric session key for encryption. The connection is now private,
              authentic, and tamper-proof.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>4.3 Types of SSL Certificates</h3>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Domain Validated (DV)</h4>
          <p>The most basic level. Proves you control the domain — nothing more. Issued within minutes, often free.
            Shows a padlock. Suitable for blogs, portfolios.</p>
          <span class="badge">Free to Low Cost</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Organization Validated (OV)</h4>
          <p>The Certificate Authority verifies both domain ownership <em>and</em> organization identity (legal business
            name, address). Takes 1–3 days. Shows company name in certificate details.</p>
          <span class="badge badge-warn">$50–$300/year</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Extended Validation (EV)</h4>
          <p>The strictest validation. CA verifies legal existence, operational address, phone, and identity in person
            (or by documentation). Maximum trust for banks, e-commerce.</p>
          <span class="badge badge-success">$150–$700/year</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Wildcard SSL</h4>
          <p>Covers a domain AND all its subdomains: <code>*.example.com</code> secures <code>blog.example.com</code>,
            <code>shop.example.com</code>, <code>api.example.com</code> — all with one certificate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Multi-Domain (SAN) SSL</h4>
          <p>One certificate covers multiple completely different domains: <code>example.com</code> +
            <code>mysite.net</code> + <code>anotherbrand.org</code>. Efficient for businesses managing many brands.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>4.4 Free SSL — Let's Encrypt</h3>
      <p><strong>Let's Encrypt</strong> is a non-profit Certificate Authority launched in 2016 that provides free,
        automated DV SSL certificates. Before it existed, SSL certificates cost hundreds of dollars per year — a
        significant barrier for small sites. Today, Let's Encrypt issues over 300 million active certificates and is
        used by a majority of the web. Most hosting providers integrate it directly into their control panels with
        one-click installation.</p>

      <h3>4.5 Why SSL Is Now Mandatory</h3>
      <ul class="styled">
        <li><strong>Google Chrome shows "Not Secure"</strong> warnings on HTTP sites, which damages visitor trust and
          bounce rates dramatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Search ranking:</strong> HTTPS is an official ranking signal. HTTP sites rank lower in search
          results.</li>
        <li><strong>HTTP/2 requires HTTPS:</strong> The faster modern web protocol (HTTP/2) only works over encrypted
          connections.</li>
        <li><strong>PCI Compliance:</strong> Any website processing credit card payments <em>must</em> use HTTPS by law.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Password &amp; form security:</strong> Without HTTPS, login forms and contact forms send data in
          plaintext — readable by anyone on the same network (e.g., public Wi-Fi).</li>
        <li><strong>User trust:</strong> 85% of users say they won't purchase from a site that doesn't show a padlock
          (Google/Harris poll).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4.6 How to Install SSL on Your Website</h3>
      <div class="step-list">
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">01</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Choose Your Certificate</h5>
            <p>For most sites, Let's Encrypt (free DV) is perfect. For business sites, consider OV. For
              finance/e-commerce, consider EV.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">02</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Generate a CSR</h5>
            <p>Create a Certificate Signing Request (CSR) — a block of encrypted text containing your site's details —
              from your server or hosting panel.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">03</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Submit &amp; Validate</h5>
            <p>Submit the CSR to your CA. For DV, prove domain ownership by adding a DNS TXT record or uploading a
              verification file. For OV/EV, submit documentation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">04</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Install the Certificate</h5>
            <p>Once issued, install the certificate files on your web server (Apache, Nginx) or via your hosting control
              panel's SSL manager.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-item">
          <div class="step-num">05</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h5>Force HTTPS Redirect</h5>
            <p>Configure your server to redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS automatically, ensuring no visitor
              accidentally uses the insecure version.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout callout-tip">
        <strong>Quick Check:</strong> Use <code>https://www.ssllabs.com/ssltest/</code> to instantly test your SSL
        configuration and get a grade (A+ to F) with specific recommendations.
      </div>

      <h3>4.7 Putting It All Together</h3>
      <p>The three pillars — <strong>Domain, Hosting, and SSL</strong> — are like the three legs of a stool. Remove any
        one and the whole thing falls over:</p>

